======================================================================
B. Synchrony: equal differences flip in lockstep (clock complexity)
======================================================================
 #distinct differences controls how many independent flip-rhythms the
 clock has. Arithmetic progressions (all diffs multiples of a gap) and
 twin clusters (many d=2) synchronise; Sidon sets maximise complexity.

[AP gap 1: 1..7] speeds=(1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7)
   pairs=21 distinct_diffs=6  (Sidon-ratio=6/21)  min_diff=1 => MAX holdback=1/2
   consecutive(d=1) edges=6   twin(d=2) edges=5
   LRC mod-cover: covered(anti-adm)=[2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]  missed(adm->lonely t=1/m)=[]
   LRC gap=0 thr=1/8 forbidden_len=1  -> tight/boundary

[AP gap 3: 3,6,9,12,15,18,21] speeds=(3, 6, 9, 12, 15, 18, 21)
   pairs=21 distinct_diffs=6  (Sidon-ratio=6/21)  min_diff=3 => MAX holdback=1/6
   consecutive(d=1) edges=0   twin(d=2) edges=0
   LRC mod-cover: covered(anti-adm)=[2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]  missed(adm->lonely t=1/m)=[]
   LRC gap=0 thr=1/8 forbidden_len=1  -> tight/boundary

[Sidon-ish 1,2,5,11,22,33,50] speeds=(1, 2, 5, 11, 22, 33, 50)
   pairs=21 distinct_diffs=18  (Sidon-ratio=18/21)  min_diff=1 => MAX holdback=1/2
   consecutive(d=1) edges=1   twin(d=2) edges=0
   LRC mod-cover: covered(anti-adm)=[2, 3, 5]  missed(adm->lonely t=1/m)=[4, 6, 7]
   LRC gap=59/4400 thr=1/8 forbidden_len=5527/6600  -> LONELY easy (positive gap)

======================================================================
C. Holdback spectrum (persistence = 1/(2d)) for a few sets
======================================================================
 [consecutive 1..6] holdbacks (value:count, stickiest first): 1/2:5, 1/4:4, 1/6:3, 1/8:2, 1/10:1
 [twin members 3,5,11,13,17,19] holdbacks (value:count, stickiest first): 1/4:3, 1/8:1, 1/12:3, 1/16:3, 1/20:1
 [geometric 1,2,4,8,16,32] holdbacks (value:count, stickiest first): 1/2:1, 1/4:1, 1/6:1, 1/8:1, 1/12:1

SUMMARY
 holdback = 1/(2*difference): small speed-gap = sticky edge.
 Consecutive integers: min_diff=1 => MAX holdback, covers all moduli
   => anti-admissible => LRC-extremal (and minimal clock, S24).
 Twin primes (d=2) are the stickiest pairs the primes allow -- the
   prime analogue of consecutive integers -- but prime sets MISS the
   small residues (admissible) and so are LRC-easy. The tension:
   holdback wants LRC-hardness; admissibility (primes) grants ease.
